T.append(L(P+"t18","Resolution","high",
"Resolution is a refutation-based inference rule that proves a statement by adding its negation to the knowledge base (in clause form) and deriving the empty clause.",
"Resolution me hum jo prove karna hai uska negation lete hain, sab ko clause form me convert karte hain, aur resolve karte jaate hain. Agar empty clause mil gaya to contradiction aaya, matlab original statement sach tha.",
"Proof by contradiction in maths: assume the opposite, derive something impossible, so the original claim must be true.",
"KB: P, P→Q, Q→R. Prove R. Clauses: P; ¬P∨Q; ¬Q∨R; negated goal ¬R. Resolve ¬R with ¬Q∨R → ¬Q. Resolve ¬Q with ¬P∨Q → ¬P. Resolve ¬P with P → empty clause ⇒ R proved.",
["Resolution rule: from (A∨B) and (¬A∨C) infer (B∨C).","Works on clauses (CNF).","Refutation: add ¬goal, derive empty clause.","Sound and refutation-complete.","Predicate logic needs conversion to clause form and unification.","Conversion: remove →, push ¬ in, standardise variables, Skolemise ∃, drop ∀, convert to CNF."],
["Clause-form steps.","Unification and substitution.","Worked refutation."],
["Convert a formula to clauses.","Unify two literals.","Perform a resolution proof.","Explain why we negate the goal."],
["Forgetting to negate the goal.","Resolving on more than one literal pair at a time.","Skipping standardising variables apart."],
[E("conceptual","Explain resolution with algorithm.","Convert KB to clauses, negate goal, resolve pairs until empty clause."),
 E("problem","Prove R from P, P→Q, Q→R by resolution.","Resolve ¬R with ¬Q∨R, then ¬P∨Q, then P ⇒ empty clause."),
 E("explain with example","Unify P(x,f(y)) with P(a,f(b)).","Substitution {x/a, y/b}."),
 E("viva","Why is resolution called refutation procedure?","It proves by refuting (contradicting) the negated goal.")],
[Q("MCQ","Resolution derives:","The empty clause","Signals contradiction.",["True","The empty clause","The goal","A fact"]),
 Q("true/false","We add the negation of the goal.","True",""),
 Q("short answer","Resolve (A∨B) and (¬A∨C).","B∨C",""),
 Q("problem solving","Unify Q(x,b) with Q(a,y).","{x/a, y/b}",""),
 Q("comparison","Skolemisation removes…","Existential quantifiers by Skolem constants/functions.",""),
 Q("viva","Is resolution sound?","Yes, and refutation-complete.","")],
algorithmSteps=["1. Convert all KB statements to clause form (CNF).","2. Negate the goal and convert it to clauses; add to KB.","3. Select two clauses with complementary literals (unify if predicate logic).","4. Add the resolvent to the clause set.","5. If the empty clause is derived → goal proved; if no new clauses → goal not provable.","6. Repeat steps 3–5."],
complexity="Worst case exponential in the number of clauses; strategies (unit preference, set of support) improve it.",
workedExample="Clauses: (1) P (2) ¬P∨Q (3) ¬Q∨R (4) ¬R. Resolve(3,4)=¬Q; Resolve(2,¬Q)=¬P; Resolve(1,¬P)=□ ⇒ R is proved."))

T.append(L(P+"t19","Natural Deduction","medium",
"Natural deduction is a proof system with introduction and elimination rules for each logical connective, mirroring how people reason with assumptions.",
"Natural deduction me hum insaan jaise proof likhte hain: assumption lo, rules apply karo aur assumption close (discharge) karke conclusion nikalo.",
"Like writing a maths proof: 'assume P…, then Q…, therefore if P then Q'.",
"Prove P→R from P→Q and Q→R. Assume P. By →E with P→Q get Q. By →E with Q→R get R. Discharge P by →I ⇒ P→R.",
["Each connective has introduction (I) and elimination (E) rules.","→E = Modus Ponens; →I discharges an assumption.","∧I, ∧E, ∨I, ∨E, ¬I, ¬E, ⊥ rules.","Proofs are trees/lists of lines with justifications.","More human-readable than resolution but harder to automate.","Sound and complete for first-order logic."],
["Key rules: →I, →E, ∧I, ∧E.","Assumption and discharge.","Comparison with resolution."],
["Write a short natural-deduction proof.","Name the rule for each line.","Compare with resolution."],
["Using an assumption after it was discharged.","Skipping justification of lines."],
[E("conceptual","Explain natural deduction with an example.","Rules with I/E; prove P→R from P→Q, Q→R using assumption P."),
 E("difference","Natural deduction vs resolution.","ND is human-style with assumptions and many rules; resolution is a single rule on clauses suited to machines.")],
[Q("MCQ","→E is also known as:","Modus Ponens","",["Modus Tollens","Modus Ponens","Resolution","Addition"]),
 Q("true/false","→I discharges an assumption.","True",""),
 Q("short answer","What does ∧E do?","From P∧Q derive P (or Q).",""),
 Q("problem solving","Assume P, have P→Q. Derive?","Q by →E",""),
 Q("comparison","Natural deduction vs truth table.","ND derives conclusions by rules; truth table checks all interpretations.","")],
algorithmSteps=["1. Write the premises.","2. Assume the antecedent of the goal implication.","3. Apply elimination/introduction rules to derive the consequent.","4. Discharge the assumption with →I to obtain the implication."],
workedExample="1. P→Q (premise) 2. Q→R (premise) 3. P (assume) 4. Q (→E 1,3) 5. R (→E 2,4) 6. P→R (→I 3–5)."))

